Here's a Java code snippet that demonstrates how to parse HTML fragments using Jsoup and add public comments:

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;
import org.jsoup.select.Elements;

public class HtmlParser {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String inputFragment = "<html><body><p>Hello World!</p></body></html>";
        String baseUri = "http://example.com";

        Document doc = Jsoup.parse(inputFragment, baseUri);

        // add a comment before parsing the fragment
        System.out.println("Comment before parsing: ");

        // parse the HTML fragment using Jsoup
        Node result = parseHtml(doc, inputFragment, baseUri);
        System.out.println(result.toString());

        if (result != null) {
            // insert comments into the parsed HTML
            Element pElement = doc.selectFirst("p").first();
            Comment comment = new Comment();
            comment.insert(pElement.text(), pElement);

            // print updated HTML with comments
            System.out.println(comment.toString());
        } else {
            System.out.println("No HTML to parse.");
        }
    }

    public static Node parseHtml(Document doc, String inputFragment, String baseUri) {
        Elements elements = doc.getAllElements();
        for (Element element : elements) {
            if (element.tagName().equals("p")) {
                // add a comment before parsing the paragraph
                System.out.println("Comment before parsing: " + element.text());
                Comment comment = new Comment();
                comment.insert(element.text(), element);

                // insert comments into the parsed HTML
                element.insertBefore(comment, element);
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}

class Comment {
    private String text;

    public void insert(String text, Element parentElement) {
        parentElement.appendChild(document.createTextNode(text));
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Comment{" +
                "text='" + text + '\'' +
                '}';
    }
}
```

This Java code snippet demonstrates how to parse HTML fragments using Jsoup and add comments. It iterates over the elements in the parsed HTML, finds paragraphs (represented by `<p>` tags), adds a comment before parsing each paragraph, and inserts the comment into the parsed HTML.

The `Comment` class is used to represent comments in the code. It has an `insert` method that appends text to the parent element using `appendChild`. The `toString` method returns a string representation of the comment object.

In the `main` method, it creates a Jsoup document from the input HTML fragment and calls the `parseHtml` method to parse the HTML. Finally, it prints the updated HTML with comments in real-time using `insertBefore`.
